Context: this page belongs to Grothendieck's programme of 'déploiement' (unfolding) of stratified spaces announced in the Esquisse. A pair (X,Y) with Y a closed stratum and a germ of conical structure 𝒯 is encoded by unfolding data (X̃, Ẋ, ε_Ẋ, Y, p: Ẋ→Y):
- X̃ is the 'blown-up' space, a manifold-with-boundary-like object;
- Ẋ ⊂ X̃ is its boundary, the link bundle;
- ε_Ẋ is a germ of collar (cylindrical neighbourhood) of Ẋ in X̃;
- p: Ẋ → Y is the link fibration;
- X is recovered as X̃ ∪_Ẋ Cyl(p), with the conical structure coming from the collar and the mapping cylinder.

On the previous page Grothendieck defined when a cylindrical neighbourhood E ≅ X_0×I of a 'bordering' (codimension-one, two-sided-boundary-like) subset X_0 is compatible with a conical neighbourhood T of Y. The requirements are:
- T∩E ≅ T_0×I, inducing Ṫ∩E ≅ Ṫ_0×I and Y∩E ≅ Y_0×I;
- T∩E, Ṫ∩E and T_0 are conical subspaces of T;
- the homotheties λ_E and λ_T commute, so that λ_T restricts to λ_{T_0}×id_I.

This page:
(1) NB. Compatibility is stable under shrinking: for λ_0, λ'_0 ∈ ]0,1], the shrunk neighbourhoods λ_{0,E}E and λ'_{0,T}T are still compatible. So compatibility is a property of germs.
(2) If T is itself cylindrical, i.e. a collar of a bordering Y, the compatibility condition between two cylindrical structures is symmetric. This is the germ of a 'corner' structure.
(3) Proposition. Given the unfolding data of (X,Y,𝒯), germs of cylindrical structures (X_0,E) on X compatible with 𝒯 correspond bijectively to systems (X̃_0, ε̃_{X̃_0}; Y_0, ε_{Y_0}), where:
- X̃_0 is a bordering part of X̃ with a germ of collar;
- Y_0 is a bordering part of Y with a germ of collar;
- (a) ε̃_{X̃_0} and ε_Ẋ are compatible cylindrical germs along Ẋ_0 = X̃_0∩Ẋ, the boundary trace of X̃_0, which receives an induced collar inside Ẋ. In modern terms this is a codimension-2 corner where two collars commute;
- (b) p is compatible with the collars: Ẋ_0 = p^{-1}(Y_0), and there exist representative collars E_Ẋ ≅ Ẋ_0×I and E_Y ≅ Y_0×I on which p restricts to p_0×id_I (continued on the next page).

The next page generalises to finite 'transversal' families of collars (B_i, E_{B_i}). Their intersections are modelled as B_J×I^J with independent commuting homotheties, i.e. a manifold-with-corners/control-data formalism. Purpose: to make the unfolding functorial with respect to sub-stratifications and boundaries, and to set up an induction on strata.
